# x is a nat variable equal to 1 and y a real one.  In y := x / 2 + 0.5 the operands of / are
# integers, so the quotient is integer division, 0, and y is 0.5: the probability that
# y >= 1 is 0, and the lower bound 1 on it is false.  (The post is an indicator because an
# expectation must be non-negative in every state, which the real y is not.)
# Expected: unknown (the bound is false).  When the mixed sum was read over the reals, x was
# converted to a real before the division, y was 1, and the false bound was proved.
nat x;
real y;
x := 1;
y := x / 2 + 0.5;
?Ex [[y >= 1]] >= 1
